# Throwing exceptions & custom types

## `throw` — fail fast at the boundary

```java
void setAge(int age) {
    if (age < 0)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("age must be >= 0, got " + age);
    this.age = age;
}
```

Reject bad input *where it enters*, with the offending value in the message. Prefer the standard types: `IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`, `Objects.requireNonNull(x, "x")`.

## Define a custom type — when the failure is domain-meaningful

Extend an exception; the branch you pick sets checked vs unchecked:

```java
class OrderNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {   // unchecked: a bug
    OrderNotFoundException(String id) { super("no order " + id); }
}

class InsufficientFundsException extends Exception {       // checked: recoverable
    final BigDecimal shortfall;
    // ... super(msg); carry data callers can act on
}
```

- **Pass a message to `super`** — self-explanatory stack trace
- **Carry data** (`shortfall()`) so handlers act on specifics

## The defining choice

- `RuntimeException` → a **bug** (default; don't force a catch)
- `Exception` → a **recoverable** condition callers must confront (use sparingly)
